WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.568 --> 00:00:03.636
-Our first guest tonight is an
Academy Award- an Emmy-nominated

00:00:03.636 --> 00:00:07.040
actor and musician who you know
and love from his work in movies

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:09.609
such as "Thor: Ragnarok"
and "Independence Day"

00:00:09.609 --> 00:00:12.345
and his series "The World
According to Jeff Goldblum."

00:00:12.345 --> 00:00:14.914
He returns to the "Jurassic"
franchise

00:00:14.914 --> 00:00:16.282
in "Jurassic World: Dominion,"

00:00:16.282 --> 00:00:18.250
which is in theaters
and IMAX June 10th.

00:00:18.250 --> 00:00:19.652
Please welcome back to the show

00:00:19.652 --> 00:00:21.888
our good friend Jeff Goldblum,
everybody.

00:00:21.888 --> 00:00:23.656
♪♪
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:23.656 --> 00:00:30.897 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:30.897 --> 00:00:38.171 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:38.171 --> 00:00:45.411 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:45.411 --> 00:00:48.013
Welcome back.
-Thank you so much.

00:00:48.013 --> 00:00:51.050
-This is what -- You know,
we've often talked about

00:00:51.050 --> 00:00:52.652
our shared connection to
Pittsburgh,

00:00:52.652 --> 00:00:55.121
and I feel like you're wearing
a Steelers-themed sweatshirt.

00:00:55.121 --> 00:00:56.422
-That's so funny
you should say that.

00:00:56.422 --> 00:00:58.291
I was going to draw your
attention to it if it came up.

00:00:58.291 --> 00:01:01.060
Yes. How about that?
Yellow and gold.

00:01:01.060 --> 00:01:03.296
And in honor of the day

00:01:03.296 --> 00:01:06.165
they will crush
the Green Bay Packers.

00:01:06.165 --> 00:01:07.834
-Oh, that's a lovely --
-You see?

00:01:07.834 --> 00:01:10.970
-You're very -- Yeah, it's like
V-ing out Green Bay, yes.

00:01:10.970 --> 00:01:13.506
-That's right.
-I love it. Very nice.

00:01:13.506 --> 00:01:15.741
How are you?
-Look at me. I'm good.

00:01:15.741 --> 00:01:16.976
-I think one of the --

00:01:16.976 --> 00:01:18.978
[ Laughter ]

00:01:18.978 --> 00:01:22.548
Now, I've added a little girl,
but we've always talked

00:01:22.548 --> 00:01:24.884
in the past -- we have boys that
are almost the exact same ages.

00:01:24.884 --> 00:01:26.686
-Yes, that's right.
How about that?

00:01:26.686 --> 00:01:29.322
Yeah, ours is almost seven.

00:01:29.322 --> 00:01:31.623
Charlie is,
and then River is five.

00:01:31.623 --> 00:01:32.992
-That's fantastic.
-How about that?

00:01:32.992 --> 00:01:34.760
And you added of course
eight months ago --

00:01:34.760 --> 00:01:35.995
-Yeah, we added a little girl.

00:01:35.995 --> 00:01:37.763
Now, this is a question
I have for you

00:01:37.763 --> 00:01:40.566
because you're teaching
your boys how to play piano.

00:01:40.566 --> 00:01:42.735
-Well, I'm not really, but I'll
tell you all about it, yes.

00:01:42.735 --> 00:01:43.836
-Okay, great.
-Yeah.

00:01:43.836 --> 00:01:46.138
-But you know how, right?
-Somewhat.

00:01:46.138 --> 00:01:48.140
I'm a humble student.
I like to practice, yeah.

00:01:48.140 --> 00:01:50.309
-See, because that's the thing.
Like, my wife and I,

00:01:50.309 --> 00:01:53.046
neither of us are musical,
so I don't know if we're ready

00:01:53.046 --> 00:01:54.613
to take a leap like this with
our children

00:01:54.613 --> 00:01:56.516
because neither of us
have any skills.

00:01:56.516 --> 00:01:58.417
-Well, it's a good question.

00:01:58.417 --> 00:02:01.854
I didn't realize -- I didn't
imagine it when I signed them up

00:02:01.854 --> 00:02:06.326
for piano lessons that we would
actually -- it would fall to us

00:02:06.326 --> 00:02:09.228
to be the facilitators,
the rehearsal directors

00:02:09.228 --> 00:02:11.230
because my parents
did not do that.

00:02:11.230 --> 00:02:14.133
And so the answer to your
question is you needn't wait.

00:02:14.133 --> 00:02:17.136
You don't have to -- I mean,
they'll have a teacher, right?

00:02:17.136 --> 00:02:19.806
And then, I don't know,
it's up to them to kind of --

00:02:19.806 --> 00:02:21.173
you can help out if you can.

00:02:21.173 --> 00:02:22.708
I'm sure you can early on.

00:02:22.708 --> 00:02:27.680
You know how to -- And I believe
the Suzuki method suggests

00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:30.216
that the parent kind of learns
along with.

00:02:30.216 --> 00:02:31.584
-See, I don't want to do that.

00:02:31.584 --> 00:02:33.686
-Oh.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:33.686 --> 00:02:35.889
Well, then --
-I feel like the window's closed

00:02:35.889 --> 00:02:37.389
'cause here's what I'm thinking.

00:02:37.389 --> 00:02:39.592
I feel like if my kids
and I learn at the same time,

00:02:39.592 --> 00:02:43.429
they will lap me so quickly with
their, like, with their little

00:02:43.429 --> 00:02:45.497
baby fingers that are
growing and learning,

00:02:45.497 --> 00:02:47.566
whereas mine are
just getting less.

00:02:47.566 --> 00:02:48.701
-Yes, of course.
-Yeah.

00:02:48.701 --> 00:02:50.770
-Well, that's our destiny,
isn't it?

00:02:50.770 --> 00:02:52.705
The children will go like this

00:02:52.705 --> 00:02:55.641
and we go like that
in every way.

00:02:55.641 --> 00:02:57.610
My kids already outrun me.
-Yeah.

00:02:57.610 --> 00:02:59.345
-You know,
"Dada, here, let's race."

00:02:59.345 --> 00:03:01.180
You know, I can't outrace them.

00:03:01.180 --> 00:03:04.250
And they wrestle me to
the ground

00:03:04.250 --> 00:03:07.953
and beat me into a pulp
whenever they like.

00:03:07.953 --> 00:03:09.656
Oh, my gosh.
-It's tough.

00:03:09.656 --> 00:03:10.956
Well, let's be honest, Jeff.

00:03:10.956 --> 00:03:12.992
We're slightly, I believe,
probably slightly older

00:03:12.992 --> 00:03:15.194
than the dads that both of our
kids are friends with, right?

00:03:15.194 --> 00:03:17.964
-Not you.
You're younger than springtime.

00:03:17.964 --> 00:03:20.699
-I am a little bit older than
the other dads at the school,

00:03:20.699 --> 00:03:22.969
and I want to impress that
upon my children.

00:03:22.969 --> 00:03:24.971
I'm like, "Don't think you can
wrestle me the way

00:03:24.971 --> 00:03:26.271
these children wrestle
their father."

00:03:26.271 --> 00:03:27.406
[ Laughter ]
-Really? Really?

00:03:27.406 --> 00:03:28.874
-Yeah, I'm like,
"This is a different thing."

00:03:28.874 --> 00:03:31.544
-Really? Yeah, yeah.

00:03:31.544 --> 00:03:34.647
I know, I know. Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:34.647 --> 00:03:36.683
-But this is very exciting.

00:03:36.683 --> 00:03:39.285
This is gonna be the first time
your kids are going to see

00:03:39.285 --> 00:03:41.454
a movie on the big screen
which is "Jurassic."

00:03:41.454 --> 00:03:43.389
-Yes, that's true.
This "Jurassic World: Dominion"

00:03:43.389 --> 00:03:46.358
movie is coming out tomorrow,
and they've never --

00:03:46.358 --> 00:03:48.994
Neither of them have seen
a movie in a movie theater.

00:03:48.994 --> 00:03:50.797
-My kids haven't either.
-Ah.

00:03:50.797 --> 00:03:52.632
-Yeah.
-So, you know, there was COVID

00:03:52.632 --> 00:03:55.267
and we've been selective about
what we wanted to show them,

00:03:55.267 --> 00:03:57.103
but that's a whole other
interesting conversation to me,

00:03:57.103 --> 00:03:59.872
what movies at what point --
-See, I only --

00:03:59.872 --> 00:04:01.607
We only show them this show.

00:04:01.607 --> 00:04:03.576
[ Laughter ]

00:04:03.576 --> 00:04:05.143
-Good idea.
-Yeah, they don't --

00:04:05.143 --> 00:04:08.781
They hate it, but we're like,
"This pays the bills!"

00:04:08.781 --> 00:04:12.985
-Exactly. So you're like Malcolm
McDowell in "Clockwork Orange."

00:04:12.985 --> 00:04:14.988
-Yeah, very.
Very "Clockwork Orange."

00:04:14.988 --> 00:04:16.355
-Yeah, get their eyes open.

00:04:16.355 --> 00:04:18.891
-They're much better, you know,
these days obviously.

00:04:18.891 --> 00:04:20.292
They're less metal,
but they're very good.

00:04:20.292 --> 00:04:22.428
-Of course. Oh, still.
You can pry them open.

00:04:22.428 --> 00:04:25.531
Oh, yeah. That's right.
-Are they excited?

00:04:25.531 --> 00:04:27.166
Do they know they're going to
see it on a big screen?

00:04:27.166 --> 00:04:30.869
-I keep telling them,
yes, and we're going --

00:04:30.869 --> 00:04:31.871
I say, "It's going to be loud."

00:04:31.871 --> 00:04:33.406
We're going to see
an IMAX thing,

00:04:33.406 --> 00:04:34.807
as you commented
in the monologue --

00:04:34.807 --> 00:04:37.843
"and it's going to be loud
and it's kind of scary."

00:04:37.843 --> 00:04:40.880
But, you know, you know --
we'll see what happens.

00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:43.382
-Have you ever small-screen
shown them the original?

00:04:43.382 --> 00:04:46.586
-Yeah, I did a little bit ago
this last year or two,

00:04:46.586 --> 00:04:48.388
"Jurassic Park"
and the second one.

00:04:48.388 --> 00:04:51.391
And the little one was kind of,
"Oh, no," duh-duh-duh-duh.

00:04:51.391 --> 00:04:54.293
But they were into it and now
they can't get enough of it.

00:04:54.293 --> 00:04:57.863
They're very -- Well, any TV
really is mesmerizing

00:04:57.863 --> 00:05:00.265
and addictive immediately.

00:05:00.265 --> 00:05:03.235
-I don't know if you had this
thing because we waited so long

00:05:03.235 --> 00:05:04.770
to introduce films to them

00:05:04.770 --> 00:05:07.205
that we were showing them
"Paddington," you know?

00:05:07.205 --> 00:05:09.641
And all those movies, there's
a moment where, you know,

00:05:09.641 --> 00:05:11.544
the hero is, you know,
Paddington gets kicked out of

00:05:11.544 --> 00:05:13.713
the house for some bull[bleep],
let's be honest.

00:05:13.713 --> 00:05:15.381
[ Laughter ]

00:05:15.381 --> 00:05:17.216
-I'm not familiar with any
Paddington.

00:05:17.216 --> 00:05:19.584
-Yeah, but, like, Paddington's
out in the rain and my kids,

00:05:19.584 --> 00:05:21.521
both my kids are sobbing.

00:05:21.521 --> 00:05:23.823
And I wanted to be like, "Oh."
I said to my wife, I'm like,

00:05:23.823 --> 00:05:25.258
"We haven't, like, taught them
about movies.

00:05:25.258 --> 00:05:26.959
We got to be like,
'Look, Paddington

00:05:26.959 --> 00:05:29.494
is going to be fine.'"
[ Laughter ]

00:05:29.494 --> 00:05:31.797
-That's very interesting,
though.

00:05:31.797 --> 00:05:34.032
No, I think that's emotionally
sophisticated, isn't it?

00:05:34.032 --> 00:05:36.001
I remember the first movie that
I ever cried,

00:05:36.001 --> 00:05:38.704
that made me cry was
a Jackie Gleason movie.

00:05:38.704 --> 00:05:41.841
He played a kind of a mute --
"Gigot."

00:05:41.841 --> 00:05:43.676
But I think that was
a little later.

00:05:43.676 --> 00:05:44.944
I think that's so interesting.

00:05:44.944 --> 00:05:48.014
But I don't think Charlie
has ever cried at a movie.

00:05:48.014 --> 00:05:50.349
The older one, although there's
much wailing and gnashing

00:05:50.349 --> 00:05:51.617
of teeth, as you can imagine.

00:05:51.617 --> 00:05:53.719
the emotional roller coaster
is ever present.

00:05:53.719 --> 00:05:57.323
-I mean, I should say that
I love my kids' sensitivity.

00:05:57.323 --> 00:05:59.025
It's a wonderful thing.
-Sensitivity?

00:05:59.025 --> 00:06:01.493
-Yeah, it's great.
I also want to be like,

00:06:01.493 --> 00:06:03.563
"When you're watching these
movies with your friends..."

00:06:03.563 --> 00:06:04.696
-Yeah.
-"...you got to keep

00:06:04.696 --> 00:06:05.898
your [bleep] together when
Paddington --"

00:06:05.898 --> 00:06:07.667
[ Laughter ]
-No. Don't say that.

00:06:07.667 --> 00:06:10.269
-"When Paddington goes outside,
you can't be like, 'Aah!'

00:06:10.269 --> 00:06:13.673
'cause the other kids -- the
other kids know he's coming --

00:06:13.673 --> 00:06:15.108
-Yeah, yeah.
-This isn't like --

00:06:15.108 --> 00:06:17.109
It's gonna be like, "Is this the
movie where Paddington dies?"

00:06:17.109 --> 00:06:19.312
I'm like, "That doesn't --
That movie doesn't exist."

00:06:19.312 --> 00:06:20.646
[ Laughter ]

00:06:20.646 --> 00:06:23.582
-Well, again, I don't know about
Paddington, but -- but --

00:06:23.582 --> 00:06:26.119
-You're being very negative
about Paddington today.

00:06:26.119 --> 00:06:28.521
-No, I'm not.
I just want to be honest.

00:06:28.521 --> 00:06:31.223
But they've seen me
cry at movies, though.

00:06:31.223 --> 00:06:32.458
I kind of --
-Oh, really?

00:06:32.458 --> 00:06:35.394
-Yes, yes. And sometimes
I get "boo-hoo-hoo."

00:06:35.394 --> 00:06:39.899
I get very vociferous
and, you know, they're a little

00:06:39.899 --> 00:06:42.534
bit interested, and then
sometimes they go,

00:06:42.534 --> 00:06:43.903
"Dada, stop it, stop it."

00:06:43.903 --> 00:06:45.204
-Oh, yeah.
-They kind of do that.

00:06:45.204 --> 00:06:47.306
-I will say, since I had kids,
I cry all the time.

00:06:47.306 --> 00:06:50.209
Like, I feel like I'm more
sensitive now as well.

00:06:50.209 --> 00:06:52.244
-Well, I know.
I mean, it just breaks your --

00:06:52.244 --> 00:06:54.780
It opens up -- cracks your heart
way open.

00:06:54.780 --> 00:06:56.349
-Yeah.
-They do things, just looking at

00:06:56.349 --> 00:06:58.584
them, looking at pictures
and things, I can --

00:06:58.584 --> 00:07:00.186
-Yeah, well, you and me both.

00:07:00.186 --> 00:07:01.487
I have a lot more questions
for you.

00:07:01.487 --> 00:07:03.556
Will you stick around?
-Yes, I want to very badly.

00:07:03.556 --> 00:07:05.858
-Alright, great. We'll be
right back with more
from Jeff Goldblum.

